 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
 *   **Norm Selection:** Using $p=2$ (Euclidean norm) results in a spherical contraction within a cubic grid, wasting approximately 50% of the grid points because a sphere occupies only a fraction of a cube [S2, unofficial]. Using $p=\infty$ creates a cuboid contracted space, utilizing the grid more efficiently [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Boundedness Assumption:** Standard Occupancy Grids (e.g., from Instant-NGP) only support bounded scenes. Scene contraction is required to extend these structures to unbounded scenes [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Gaussian Contraction Complexity:** Using the full multivariate Gaussian contraction method from Mip-NeRF 360 is computationally expensive and overly expressive for isotropic Gaussians. The Jacobian geometric mean method is preferred [S1].
